    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Does an under desk elliptical count as steps? Not on its own, and not accurately on most wrist trackers. Your watch or band counts steps by detecting the rhythm of your arm swinging. When you pedal under a desk, your arms stay on the keyboard, so the tracker sees almost nothing.</p>
<p>That does not mean the movement is wasted. Your legs are doing real work and burning extra calories. You just need a different way to record it: move the tracker to your ankle, use a shoe pod, or log the session as a workout. Below we explain how each method works, and how to convert your pedaling minutes into a fair step equivalent.</p>

<h2>Why Most Trackers Don't Count Under Desk Elliptical Steps</h2>
<p>Wrist-worn fitness trackers and smartwatches use an accelerometer to sense motion. Their step algorithms look for the repeating up-and-down and forward-and-back pattern your wrist makes when you walk. That pattern comes from your arms swinging in time with your legs.</p>
<p>Seated pedaling breaks the link. Your legs move, but your wrists rest on a desk, a mouse or a keyboard. Some trackers pick up a few stray "steps" from typing or shifting in your chair, but most sessions register a small fraction of the actual pedal strokes, if any.</p>
<p>Phones in a pocket have a similar problem. When you sit, the phone sits too, and it barely moves with your legs.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Quick test:</strong> note your step count, pedal for five minutes at a steady pace, and check again. If the count barely moved, your tracker is not seeing your pedaling.</p></div>

<h2>How to Make an Under Desk Elliptical Count as Steps</h2>
<h3>1. Move your tracker to your ankle</h3>
<p>This is the most common trick. Slip a band-style tracker into your sock, attach it to your ankle with a stretchy band, or clip it near your shoelaces. Now the accelerometer moves with your leg, and each pedal stroke looks a lot like a step.</p>
<p>Slim trackers work best because they sit flat against the ankle. A [[amazon:fitbit-inspire-3|Fitbit Inspire 3]] is a popular choice for this because it is small and the tracker module can be clipped or banded easily. Move it back to your wrist afterward so your sleep and heart-rate data stay accurate.</p>
<h3>2. Use a shoe pod or clip-on pedometer</h3>
<p>A small clip-on pedometer or shoe pod does the same job without moving your main tracker. Clip it to your shoelaces or sock while you pedal and add the count to your daily total by hand. It is cheap and keeps your watch on your wrist. You can [[search:clip on pedometer|browse clip-on pedometers on Amazon]].</p>
<h3>3. Log the session as a workout</h3>
<p>Most tracker apps let you start or add an exercise. Choose an elliptical, cycling or general workout type and enter the minutes. You may not get steps, but you usually get credit as active or exercise minutes, and the calorie burn is included in your daily total.</p>
<p>A tracker with continuous heart rate, such as the [[amazon:fitbit-charge-6|Fitbit Charge 6]], is the best fit for this approach. It estimates effort from your heart rate rather than wrist motion, so a harder pedaling session shows up as harder.</p>
<h3>4. Use the machine's own counter</h3>
<p>Many under desk ellipticals show strides or revolutions on a small display, and some sync with an app. The [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]] adds Bluetooth syncing with the Cubii app. You can treat strides as a rough step proxy, but see the caveat in the next section.</p>

<h2>Tips by Tracker Type</h2>
<p>Apps and menus change often, so check your device's current help pages, but these general approaches work across the major brands.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Counts pedaling as steps on the wrist?</th><th>Best workaround</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fitbit band (Inspire, Charge)</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Ankle placement for steps, or log an exercise for active minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Apple Watch</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Start a workout so the session counts toward exercise minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Garmin and other sport watches</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Record an indoor cardio or cycling activity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phone in a pocket</td><td>Almost never while seated</td><td>Add the session manually in your health app</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clip-on pedometer</td><td>Yes, if clipped to the shoe or ankle</td><td>Add its count to your daily total by hand</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Whatever you use, pick one method and stick with it. Switching between ankle counts, workout logs and estimates makes it hard to see whether you are actually moving more week to week.</p>

<h2>How to Build a Daily Routine Around Steps and Pedaling</h2>
<p>The most practical approach for desk workers is to combine both. Walk when you can, and pedal when you have to sit. A simple day might look like this:</p>
<ol>
<li>A 10 to 15 minute walk before work or at lunch for real steps.</li>
<li>Pedaling blocks of 20 to 30 minutes during email, reading and calls.</li>
<li>A short walking break every hour or two to stand up and stretch.</li>
</ol>
<p>Short blocks are easier to keep up than marathon sessions. Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> can remind you when to start and stop. For machine setup, chair height and keeping the unit from sliding, read <a href="/how-to-use-under-desk-elliptical/">how to use an under desk elliptical</a>. A [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] is worth adding if your floor is smooth.</p>

<h2>Are Pedal Strokes the Same as Steps?</h2>
<p>Not quite. A walking step means lifting and carrying your whole body weight forward. A seated pedal stroke moves only your leg against resistance. So 1,000 pedal strokes take noticeably less energy than 1,000 walking steps, especially at light resistance.</p>
<p>The muscles involved differ too. Walking loads the glutes and calves through your body weight, while seated pedaling is mostly a quadriceps exercise. Our guide to <a href="/what-muscles-does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">what muscles an under desk elliptical works</a> breaks this down.</p>
<p>That is why the ankle trick can flatter you. Pedal briskly for an hour at low resistance and your ankle tracker may report several thousand "steps," far more than the effort deserves. That is fine for motivation, but if you want an honest comparison with walking, use an effort-matched conversion instead.</p>

<h2>How Many Steps Is an Under Desk Elliptical Worth?</h2>
<p>The fairest approach compares energy cost. Brisk walking at about 3 mph is roughly 3.5 METs in the Compendium of Physical Activities and works out to about 100 steps a minute. Seated pedaling is about 2.0 METs at a light pace, 2.8 at moderate and 3.5 when you push hard. Comparing only the energy you burn above sitting still (about 1.3 METs) gives these step equivalents:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Minutes pedaled</th><th>Light (about 32 steps/min)</th><th>Moderate (about 68 steps/min)</th><th>Hard (about 100 steps/min)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>15 minutes</td><td>about 480</td><td>about 1,020</td><td>about 1,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>30 minutes</td><td>about 950</td><td>about 2,050</td><td>about 3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>60 minutes</td><td>about 1,900</td><td>about 4,100</td><td>about 6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 hours</td><td>about 3,800</td><td>about 8,200</td><td>about 12,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Use the converter to plug in your own minutes and see how close you get to your daily step goal:</p>
